format 1
adapter safety 1
section: myths

card safety-myth-exposed-radioactive
title: Myth: exposure makes you radioactive
icon: CloseCircle
entry: exposure
references: ref-cdc-contamination-vs-exposure
references: ref-nrc-radiation-faq
summary: Being exposed to radiation does not make a person radioactive.
steps: Myth: a person who was exposed to radiation is now radioactive.
steps: What is true: being exposed to radiation does not make one radioactive.
steps: What is true: an x-ray exposes a person to radiation, but does not contaminate them.
steps: Radioactive material on the skin or clothes is the concern. It is washed off.

card safety-myth-ki-all-radiation
title: Myth: KI pills block all radiation
icon: CloseCircle
references: ref-cdc-potassium-iodide
summary: Potassium iodide protects one organ from one kind of radioactive material.
steps: Myth: potassium iodide pills protect you from radiation in general.
steps: What is true: KI protects only the thyroid and only against radioactive iodine.
steps: What is true: it cannot reverse damage already done, and may not give 100% protection.
steps: Take KI only if officials or a healthcare provider tell you to.

card safety-myth-microwave-radiation
title: Myth: microwaves are nuclear radiation
icon: Wifi
entry: ionizing-radiation
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-nrc-radiation-faq
summary: Microwaves and radio waves are non-ionizing. Nuclear radiation is ionizing.
steps: Myth: microwave radiation is the same kind of radiation as nuclear radiation.
steps: What is true: radio waves, visible light and microwaves are non-ionizing radiation.
steps: What is true: ionizing radiation has enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms, which can damage tissue and DNA.
steps: Microwaves make electrons vibrate to make heat. They cannot remove electrons from atoms.

card safety-myth-plant-explodes
title: Myth: a plant can blow up like a bomb
icon: Flash
entry: nuclear-reactor
references: ref-cdc-nuclear-blast-faq
references: ref-nrc-radiation-faq
summary: A reactor does not explode like a nuclear weapon, but an accident can release radiation.
steps: Myth: a nuclear power plant can explode like a nuclear bomb.
steps: What is true: a power plant would not explode like a nuclear weapon.
steps: What is true: its fuel is arranged so energy is produced steadily, not all at once.
steps: A serious accident could still release radiation into the air, and officials would monitor it.

card safety-myth-instantly-deadly
title: Myth: any radiation is deadly
icon: CloseCircle
entry: absorbed-dose
references: ref-epa-radiation-health-effects
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation
summary: What matters is the dose and how fast it arrives.
steps: Myth: any amount of radiation is instantly deadly.
steps: What is true: acute radiation syndrome needs a very high dose delivered over a short time.
steps: What is true: environmental levels do not cause immediate effects. Cancer risk rises with dose.
steps: Low doses delivered slowly carry a substantially lower risk, as the body can repair damage.
